ds have\nwashed out, or collapsed completely. If you live in the warned area,\nit is recommended to not travel this evening or overnight. This is a\nPARTICULARLY DANGEROUS SITUATION.\n\n* WHAT...Major flooding caused by excessive rainfall will continue.\nThis is a PARTICULARLY DANGEROUS SITUATION.\n\n* WHERE...Western Archuleta County in southwestern Colorado...\nWestern Dolores County in southwestern Colorado...\nSoutheastern La Plata County in southwestern Colorado...\nMontezuma County in southwestern Colorado...\nWest Central San Miguel County in southwestern Colorado...\nSouthern San Juan County in southeastern Utah...\n\n* WHEN...Until 415 PM MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is occurring. Numerous roads remain\nclosed due to flooding.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 359 PM MST, local law enforcement reported heavy rain in the\nwarned area. Flooding is already occurring. Between 1.5 and 3\ninches of rain have fallen.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nDurango, Blanding, Mexican Hat, Cortez, Pagosa Springs, Dolores,\nDove Creek, Ignacio, Aneth, Montezuma Creek, White Mesa, Bluff,\nTselakai Dezza, Halchita, Goulding, Hovenweep National Monument,\nTowaoc, Arriola, Yellow Jacket and Lewis.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T16:15:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; San Miguel, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.616c4025c42723c2fe6d8b10282486a4525dd113.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.616c4025c42723c2fe6d8b10282486a4525dd113.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued November 1 at 10:50AM MST until November 2 at 7:00PM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"...The National Weather Service in Grand Junction CO has issued a\nFlood Warning for the following rivers in Colorado...\n\nMancos River near Mancos affecting Montezuma County.\n\nFor the Mancos River...including Mancos...Minor flooding is forecast.\n\n* WHAT...Minor flooding is forecast.\n\n* WHERE...Mancos River near Mancos.\n\n* WHEN...From this evening to Monday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...At 6.0 feet, Minor flooding can be expected around\nMancos.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 10:00 AM MST Sunday the stage was 4.8 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 5.0 feet.\n- Forecast...The river is expected to rise above flood stage this\nevening to a crest of 6.3 feet late this evening. It will then\nfall below flood stage late tonight.\n- Flood stage is 6.0 feet.\n- Flood History...This crest compares to a previous crest of 5.7\nfeet on 05/21/2005.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T19:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Montezuma, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.0807a2df4e98bab16c60e02a74ad5937530b2c74.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.0807a2df4e98bab16c60e02a74ad5937530b2c74.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 31 at 10:37PM MDT until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and southeast\nUtah, including the following areas, Canyonlands/Natural Bridges,\nLa Sal and Abajo Mountains and Southeast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...From 5 AM MST Sunday through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recently burned areas, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- 1 to 3 inches of rain is expected across portions of southeast\nUtah and southwest Colorado from Sunday morning through Monday\nmorning over soils that are still saturated from recent rainfall\nand flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.28dceea107844d87e801d5ba537294d1bcbe9f7e.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.28dceea107844d87e801d5ba537294d1bcbe9f7e.001.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-11-01T23:17:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 012317 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Jun